// NDJSON-only spec: a declaration binds to the physical JSON KEY — a third binding path, distinct from a text
// header's names and from a columnar footer's names.
//
// See csv-declared-schema.csv-spec (esql-datasource-csv/qa) for how a declaration is modelled in a `dataset:`
// directive and why `required_capability: dataset_declared_schema` must never appear.
//
// employees_no_mv.ndjson is generated from the typed-header CSV, so each record's keys are the CSV column names
// and the values carry JSON types: emp_no a number, first_name a string, salary a number.
//
// Every declared `path` here names an UNDOTTED key deliberately. A dotted declared path is currently broken on
// this format — `prepareSchema` turns it into nested traversal because `hasDottedPrefixConflict` consults only
// the declaration and never the file — so dotted binding gets a case once that is fixed, not before.

// Overlay (`dynamic: true`): the declaration retypes one key and inference supplies every other key. `salary` is
// declared without a `path`, so it binds to the key of the same name — the declaration's only effect is the type.
overlayDeclaredSchemaRetypesOneKeyOnNdjson
required_capability: dataset_in_from_command
dataset: employees_overlay_ndjson: "{{employees_no_mv}}" WITH {"mappings": {"dynamic": "true", "properties": {"salary": {"type": "double"}}}}

FROM employees_overlay_ndjson
| KEEP emp_no, first_name, salary
| SORT emp_no
| LIMIT 3;

emp_no:integer | first_name:keyword | salary:double
10001          | "Georgi"           | 57305.0
10002          | "Bezalel"          | 56371.0
10003          | "Parto"            | 61805.0
;

// Binding a dotted physical key, and the condition under which it works. NdJsonPageDecoder.prepareSchema asks
// hasDottedPrefixConflict whether some OTHER column in the schema is the dotted name's prefix: if so the name is
// read as a literal flat key, otherwise it is split on "." and read as nested traversal. The check consults the
// SCHEMA, never the file -- and under a strict declaration the schema IS the declaration. This fixture carries
// "languages" and "languages.long" as sibling literal flat keys, so declaring both makes the conflict fire and the
// dotted key binds literally.
//
// Declaring the dotted column WITHOUT its prefix is the failing shape, tracked as esql-planning#1467: no conflict
// is found, traversal into a "languages" object begins, and the read fails with a parsing_exception blaming the
// data ("value is a number, but [/languages] resolved to an object") when the file is in fact well formed. That
// case is deliberately not written here -- it would be a red test for a known defect. Add it, and promote the
// dotted case in parquet-declared-binding.csv-spec into the shared spec, when #1467 is fixed.
strictDeclaredSchemaBindsDottedKeyWhenPrefixAlsoDeclaredOnNdjson
required_capability: dataset_in_from_command
dataset: employees_declared_dotted_nd: "{{employees_no_mv}}" WITH {"mappings": {"dynamic": "false", "properties": {"id": {"type": "long", "path": "emp_no"}, "languages": {"type": "long"}, "langs": {"type": "long", "path": "languages.long"}}}}

FROM employees_declared_dotted_nd
| SORT id
| LIMIT 3;

id:long | languages:long | langs:long
10001   | 2              | 2
10002   | 5              | 5
10003   | 4              | 4
;
